1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,904
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,084 --> 00:00:03,628
You walk into a bank,

3
00:00:03,628 --> 00:00:04,804
or open its app,

4
00:00:04,804 --> 00:00:06,495
and everything is free.

5
00:00:06,675 --> 00:00:09,169
Free checking, a free debit card,

6
00:00:09,169 --> 00:00:10,832
free online transfers.

7
00:00:11,012 --> 00:00:12,720
And yet banks are some of the

8
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:15,075
most profitable businesses in the world.

9
00:00:15,255 --> 00:00:16,846
So here's the question.

10
00:00:17,026 --> 00:00:18,000
If it's all free,

11
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,777
where does the money come from?

12
00:00:19,957 --> 00:00:22,553
Today, we're going inside the business of

13
00:00:22,553 --> 00:00:24,958
banking, and I'll show you exactly how

14
00:00:24,958 --> 00:00:26,035
banks make money,

15
00:00:26,035 --> 00:00:28,630
including the parts they don't advertise.

16
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:31,139
Banks make money in two main ways.

17
00:00:31,319 --> 00:00:33,340
They earn a spread between what they

18
00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:35,192
pay you on deposits and what they

19
00:00:35,192 --> 00:00:36,091
charge on loans.

20
00:00:36,271 --> 00:00:38,586
And they charge fees for services,

21
00:00:38,586 --> 00:00:40,288
some obvious, some quiet.

22
00:00:40,638 --> 00:00:42,245
None of this is secret,

23
00:00:42,245 --> 00:00:44,271
and banks provide real value.

24
00:00:44,451 --> 00:00:45,932
They keep your money safe,

25
00:00:45,932 --> 00:00:47,983
move it around the world in seconds,

26
00:00:47,983 --> 00:00:50,034
and make it possible for millions of

27
00:00:50,034 --> 00:00:52,369
people to buy homes and start businesses.

28
00:00:52,549 --> 00:00:54,894
But understanding how they profit tells you

29
00:00:54,894 --> 00:00:56,965
exactly where you might be overpaying,

30
00:00:56,965 --> 00:00:58,982
and where your money could be working

31
00:00:58,982 --> 00:01:00,836
much harder for you instead of for

32
00:01:00,836 --> 00:01:01,327
the bank.

33
00:01:01,677 --> 00:01:03,824
We'll look at it from three angles.

34
00:01:04,004 --> 00:01:05,213
First, the numbers:

35
00:01:05,213 --> 00:01:07,504
the eight main ways banks earn money

36
00:01:07,504 --> 00:01:09,031
from customers like you.

37
00:01:09,211 --> 00:01:10,737
Second, the psychology:

38
00:01:10,737 --> 00:01:12,794
why we stay with banks that pay

39
00:01:12,794 --> 00:01:14,784
us little and charge us a lot.

40
00:01:14,964 --> 00:01:16,342
And third, the playbook:

41
00:01:16,342 --> 00:01:18,178
ten moves to make sure your bank

42
00:01:18,178 --> 00:01:18,981
works for you.

43
00:01:19,332 --> 00:01:19,884
By the end,

44
00:01:19,884 --> 00:01:21,389
you might want to look at your

45
00:01:21,389 --> 00:01:22,443
savings account rate.

46
00:01:22,623 --> 00:01:24,364
Many people are shocked by it.

47
00:01:24,544 --> 00:01:26,240
Let's start with the numbers.

48
00:01:26,590 --> 00:01:28,679
Every number here comes from a named

49
00:01:28,679 --> 00:01:29,086
source.

50
00:01:29,266 --> 00:01:32,463
Industry data comes from the Federal Deposit

51
00:01:32,463 --> 00:01:34,788
Insurance Corporation, the FDIC,

52
00:01:34,788 --> 00:01:37,985
which publishes reports on bank earnings and

53
00:01:37,985 --> 00:01:40,237
national average deposit rates.

54
00:01:40,417 --> 00:01:43,491
Overdraft data comes from the Consumer Financial

55
00:01:43,491 --> 00:01:44,643
Protection Bureau.

56
00:01:44,823 --> 00:01:46,899
Rules on debit card fees come from

57
00:01:46,899 --> 00:01:48,120
the Federal Reserve.

58
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:50,294
Interest rates change often,

59
00:01:50,294 --> 00:01:52,573
so treat any rate as a snapshot.

60
00:01:52,753 --> 00:01:54,050
This is education,

61
00:01:54,050 --> 00:01:55,562
not financial advice.

62
00:01:55,913 --> 00:01:57,063
Before the numbers,

63
00:01:57,063 --> 00:01:58,455
a quick bit of history.

64
00:01:58,635 --> 00:02:00,986
Because the way banks make money today

65
00:02:00,986 --> 00:02:02,223
is surprisingly old.

66
00:02:02,573 --> 00:02:04,511
Banking as we know it took shape

67
00:02:04,511 --> 00:02:05,359
centuries ago.

68
00:02:05,539 --> 00:02:06,967
In Renaissance Italy,

69
00:02:06,967 --> 00:02:09,822
families like the Medici of Florence built

70
00:02:09,822 --> 00:02:12,201
banking empires by taking deposits,

71
00:02:12,201 --> 00:02:15,260
lending money and moving funds between cities

72
00:02:15,260 --> 00:02:16,824
with letters of credit.

73
00:02:17,004 --> 00:02:20,824
Goldsmiths in seventeenth century London discovered something

74
00:02:20,824 --> 00:02:21,450
important.

75
00:02:21,630 --> 00:02:24,263
People left gold with them for safekeeping,

76
00:02:24,263 --> 00:02:26,039
and only a fraction of it was

77
00:02:26,039 --> 00:02:27,447
ever withdrawn at once.

78
00:02:27,627 --> 00:02:29,160
So they could lend out the rest.

79
00:02:29,510 --> 00:02:31,495
That discovery is the heart of modern

80
00:02:31,495 --> 00:02:31,924
banking.

81
00:02:32,104 --> 00:02:34,591
Keep a portion of deposits available,

82
00:02:34,591 --> 00:02:36,204
lend or invest the rest,

83
00:02:36,204 --> 00:02:37,817
and earn the difference.

84
00:02:37,997 --> 00:02:39,240
It works beautifully,

85
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:41,312
as long as everyone doesn't ask for

86
00:02:41,312 --> 00:02:43,325
their money back at the same time.

87
00:02:43,505 --> 00:02:44,350
When they do,

88
00:02:44,350 --> 00:02:45,843
it's called a bank run,

89
00:02:45,843 --> 00:02:47,662
and history is full of them.

90
00:02:48,012 --> 00:02:49,825
During the Great Depression,

91
00:02:49,825 --> 00:02:52,093
thousands of American banks failed,

92
00:02:52,093 --> 00:02:54,490
and many families lost their savings.

93
00:02:54,670 --> 00:02:58,065
In response, Congress created the Federal Deposit

94
00:02:58,065 --> 00:03:01,322
Insurance Corporation in nineteen thirty three.

95
00:03:01,502 --> 00:03:04,341
Since then, depositors at insured banks have

96
00:03:04,341 --> 00:03:06,212
been protected up to a limit,

97
00:03:06,212 --> 00:03:08,858
which today is two hundred fifty thousand

98
00:03:08,858 --> 00:03:09,374
dollars.

99
00:03:09,554 --> 00:03:11,899
The FDIC says no depositor has lost

100
00:03:11,899 --> 00:03:14,109
a penny of insured funds since it

101
00:03:14,109 --> 00:03:14,511
began.

102
00:03:14,862 --> 00:03:17,965
Banking has changed enormously since then,

103
00:03:17,965 --> 00:03:20,550
with ATMs, online banking and apps.

104
00:03:20,730 --> 00:03:22,484
But the basic model hasn't.

105
00:03:22,664 --> 00:03:25,610
Take deposits, pay depositors less than you

106
00:03:25,610 --> 00:03:27,666
earn on loans and investments,

107
00:03:27,666 --> 00:03:29,653
and charge fees for services.

108
00:03:29,833 --> 00:03:32,103
Understanding that old model is the key

109
00:03:32,103 --> 00:03:34,023
to making the modern one work for

110
00:03:34,023 --> 00:03:34,256
you.

111
00:03:34,607 --> 00:03:35,442
Angle one.

112
00:03:35,622 --> 00:03:37,608
The eight main ways banks make money.

113
00:03:37,788 --> 00:03:39,987
Let's follow your dollar after you deposit

114
00:03:39,987 --> 00:03:40,145
it.

115
00:03:40,495 --> 00:03:41,516
Way number one.

116
00:03:41,696 --> 00:03:42,567
The spread.

117
00:03:42,917 --> 00:03:44,086
When you deposit money,

118
00:03:44,086 --> 00:03:45,561
the bank doesn't keep it in a

119
00:03:45,561 --> 00:03:46,934
vault with your name on it.

120
00:03:47,114 --> 00:03:48,792
It lends much of it out,

121
00:03:48,792 --> 00:03:50,471
as mortgages, car loans,

122
00:03:50,471 --> 00:03:52,709
business loans and credit cards,

123
00:03:52,709 --> 00:03:55,436
or invests it in things like government

124
00:03:55,436 --> 00:03:55,856
bonds.

125
00:03:56,036 --> 00:03:57,800
The bank pays you a low rate

126
00:03:57,800 --> 00:03:58,808
on your deposit,

127
00:03:58,808 --> 00:04:00,698
and earns a higher rate on the

128
00:04:00,698 --> 00:04:02,085
loans and investments.

129
00:04:02,265 --> 00:04:04,575
The difference is called the net interest

130
00:04:04,575 --> 00:04:04,970
margin.

131
00:04:05,319 --> 00:04:06,754
According to the FDIC,

132
00:04:06,754 --> 00:04:09,622
the average net interest margin for American

133
00:04:09,622 --> 00:04:12,100
banks has been around three percent in

134
00:04:12,100 --> 00:04:12,947
recent years.

135
00:04:13,127 --> 00:04:14,056
That sounds small.

136
00:04:14,236 --> 00:04:16,706
But apply three percent to trillions of

137
00:04:16,706 --> 00:04:18,607
dollars of deposits and loans,

138
00:04:18,607 --> 00:04:20,887
and it becomes the largest source of

139
00:04:20,887 --> 00:04:22,851
profit in the banking industry.

140
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:25,023
Here's a simple way to picture it.

141
00:04:25,203 --> 00:04:27,736
You deposit ten thousand dollars and earn

142
00:04:27,736 --> 00:04:28,663
almost nothing.

143
00:04:28,843 --> 00:04:30,725
The bank lends that money for a

144
00:04:30,725 --> 00:04:32,303
car loan at eight percent.

145
00:04:32,483 --> 00:04:34,274
That's about eight hundred dollars a year

146
00:04:34,274 --> 00:04:34,886
on your money.

147
00:04:35,066 --> 00:04:36,185
Even after costs,

148
00:04:36,185 --> 00:04:38,621
losses and the interest they pay you,

149
00:04:38,621 --> 00:04:40,465
there's usually plenty left.

150
00:04:40,645 --> 00:04:42,363
That's the core of the business.

151
00:04:42,713 --> 00:04:43,792
Way number two.

152
00:04:43,972 --> 00:04:45,691
Paying depositors very little.

153
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:48,889
The FDIC publishes a national average rate

154
00:04:48,889 --> 00:04:50,313
for savings accounts.

155
00:04:50,493 --> 00:04:51,536
In recent years,

156
00:04:51,536 --> 00:04:54,077
even when interest rates in the economy

157
00:04:54,077 --> 00:04:55,446
were relatively high,

158
00:04:55,446 --> 00:04:58,248
that average stayed well under one percent.

159
00:04:58,428 --> 00:04:59,586
At the same time,

160
00:04:59,586 --> 00:05:02,311
many online banks and credit unions were

161
00:05:02,311 --> 00:05:05,309
paying several percent on high yield savings

162
00:05:05,309 --> 00:05:08,510
accounts, sometimes around four percent or more

163
00:05:08,510 --> 00:05:09,737
at various points.

164
00:05:10,086 --> 00:05:10,725
Why the gap?

165
00:05:10,905 --> 00:05:13,172
Many big banks don't need to compete

166
00:05:13,172 --> 00:05:14,620
hard for your deposits.

167
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:16,640
They have huge customer bases,

168
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:17,682
lots of branches,

169
00:05:17,682 --> 00:05:20,257
and customers who rarely move their money.

170
00:05:20,437 --> 00:05:21,726
So they can pay less.

171
00:05:21,906 --> 00:05:24,787
Online banks, without the cost of branches,

172
00:05:24,787 --> 00:05:27,200
often pay more to attract customers.

173
00:05:27,549 --> 00:05:29,438
Look at what that gap means in

174
00:05:29,438 --> 00:05:29,941
dollars.

175
00:05:30,121 --> 00:05:32,460
Twenty thousand dollars in an account paying

176
00:05:32,460 --> 00:05:34,373
a fraction of one percent might earn

177
00:05:34,373 --> 00:05:36,286
under one hundred dollars in a year.

178
00:05:36,466 --> 00:05:38,411
The same amount at four percent would

179
00:05:38,411 --> 00:05:40,146
earn about eight hundred dollars.

180
00:05:40,326 --> 00:05:43,252
Same safety, if both are FDIC insured.

181
00:05:43,432 --> 00:05:45,512
Seven hundred dollars of difference,

182
00:05:45,512 --> 00:05:47,418
just for the choice of where your

183
00:05:47,418 --> 00:05:48,111
savings sit.

184
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:50,982
Twenty thousand dollars in savings for one

185
00:05:50,982 --> 00:05:51,282
year.

186
00:05:51,462 --> 00:05:53,490
At a typical big bank rate,

187
00:05:53,490 --> 00:05:55,444
a fraction of one percent.

188
00:05:55,624 --> 00:05:57,692
At a four percent high yield account,

189
00:05:57,692 --> 00:05:59,258
about eight hundred dollars.

190
00:05:59,607 --> 00:06:00,687
Way number three.

191
00:06:00,867 --> 00:06:01,378
Lending.

192
00:06:01,727 --> 00:06:03,829
Lending is where the spread is earned.

193
00:06:04,009 --> 00:06:06,266
Mortgages, home equity lines,

194
00:06:06,266 --> 00:06:08,368
auto loans, personal loans,

195
00:06:08,368 --> 00:06:10,859
business loans and credit cards.

196
00:06:11,039 --> 00:06:13,381
Each carries a different rate depending on

197
00:06:13,381 --> 00:06:13,883
the risk.

198
00:06:14,063 --> 00:06:16,238
A mortgage backed by a house might

199
00:06:16,238 --> 00:06:17,453
carry a lower rate.

200
00:06:17,633 --> 00:06:19,925
A credit card with nothing behind it

201
00:06:19,925 --> 00:06:21,580
carries a much higher one,

202
00:06:21,580 --> 00:06:23,299
often above twenty percent.

203
00:06:23,648 --> 00:06:26,376
Banks also earn fees when they create

204
00:06:26,376 --> 00:06:26,818
loans.

205
00:06:26,998 --> 00:06:29,659
Origination fees, closing costs,

206
00:06:29,659 --> 00:06:31,073
application fees.

207
00:06:31,253 --> 00:06:33,520
And they can sell many mortgages to

208
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:36,369
other investors, collect a fee for servicing

209
00:06:36,369 --> 00:06:38,376
them, and use the money to lend

210
00:06:38,376 --> 00:06:38,765
again.

211
00:06:38,945 --> 00:06:41,575
A single home loan can generate income

212
00:06:41,575 --> 00:06:44,343
in several different ways over its life.

213
00:06:44,693 --> 00:06:46,592
For you, the key is that loan

214
00:06:46,592 --> 00:06:49,471
pricing depends heavily on your credit score

215
00:06:49,471 --> 00:06:50,649
and your shopping.

216
00:06:50,829 --> 00:06:53,345
Two people with similar finances can get

217
00:06:53,345 --> 00:06:54,666
very different rates,

218
00:06:54,666 --> 00:06:57,307
simply because one compared offers and the

219
00:06:57,307 --> 00:06:59,131
other accepted the first one.

220
00:06:59,311 --> 00:07:00,127
On a mortgage,

221
00:07:00,127 --> 00:07:02,226
even a small rate difference can add

222
00:07:02,226 --> 00:07:04,266
up to thousands of dollars over the

223
00:07:04,266 --> 00:07:04,616
years.

224
00:07:04,966 --> 00:07:06,035
Way number four.

225
00:07:06,215 --> 00:07:09,082
Overdraft and insufficient funds fees.

226
00:07:09,432 --> 00:07:11,676
An overdraft happens when you spend more

227
00:07:11,676 --> 00:07:13,863
than you have in your checking account,

228
00:07:13,863 --> 00:07:15,826
and the bank covers the difference,

229
00:07:15,826 --> 00:07:16,387
for a fee.

230
00:07:16,567 --> 00:07:18,950
For years, a typical fee was around

231
00:07:18,950 --> 00:07:20,517
thirty dollars or more,

232
00:07:20,517 --> 00:07:22,696
even if the purchase was a small

233
00:07:22,696 --> 00:07:23,173
coffee.

234
00:07:23,353 --> 00:07:26,495
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau found that

235
00:07:26,495 --> 00:07:29,082
banks once collected well over ten billion

236
00:07:29,082 --> 00:07:30,992
dollars a year from these fees.

237
00:07:31,342 --> 00:07:32,381
The good news:

238
00:07:32,381 --> 00:07:35,867
under pressure from regulators and competition,

239
00:07:35,867 --> 00:07:38,759
many banks have cut or eliminated these

240
00:07:38,759 --> 00:07:40,317
fees in recent years.

241
00:07:40,497 --> 00:07:43,755
The CFPB reported that overdraft and insufficient

242
00:07:43,755 --> 00:07:46,414
funds revenue fell to around six billion

243
00:07:46,414 --> 00:07:48,475
dollars in twenty twenty three,

244
00:07:48,475 --> 00:07:50,603
roughly half of what it had been

245
00:07:50,603 --> 00:07:51,933
before the pandemic.

246
00:07:52,113 --> 00:07:54,563
Still, six billion dollars is a lot

247
00:07:54,563 --> 00:07:56,872
of money, and it often comes from

248
00:07:56,872 --> 00:07:59,462
customers with the smallest balances.

249
00:07:59,811 --> 00:08:02,994
Overdraft protection is often presented as a

250
00:08:02,994 --> 00:08:03,573
service.

251
00:08:03,753 --> 00:08:05,471
And sometimes it is useful.

252
00:08:05,651 --> 00:08:07,845
But you can usually opt out of

253
00:08:07,845 --> 00:08:11,281
overdraft coverage for debit card purchases and

254
00:08:11,281 --> 00:08:14,425
ATM withdrawals, so a transaction is simply

255
00:08:14,425 --> 00:08:17,203
declined instead of costing you a fee.

256
00:08:17,383 --> 00:08:20,091
Try this: check your overdraft settings in

257
00:08:20,091 --> 00:08:21,574
your banking app today,

258
00:08:21,574 --> 00:08:24,217
and consider linking a savings account as

259
00:08:24,217 --> 00:08:25,313
a backup instead.

260
00:08:25,662 --> 00:08:29,257
According to the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau,

261
00:08:29,257 --> 00:08:32,253
banks collected around six billion dollars in

262
00:08:32,253 --> 00:08:35,381
overdraft and insufficient funds fees in twenty

263
00:08:35,381 --> 00:08:38,044
twenty three, down from more than twelve

264
00:08:38,044 --> 00:08:39,908
billion before the pandemic.

265
00:08:40,258 --> 00:08:41,302
Way number five.

266
00:08:41,482 --> 00:08:43,177
Monthly and service fees.

267
00:08:43,528 --> 00:08:45,873
Free checking often comes with conditions.

268
00:08:46,053 --> 00:08:47,652
Keep a minimum balance,

269
00:08:47,652 --> 00:08:49,182
set up direct deposit,

270
00:08:49,182 --> 00:08:51,615
or make a number of debit purchases

271
00:08:51,615 --> 00:08:52,380
each month.

272
00:08:52,560 --> 00:08:53,731
Miss the condition,

273
00:08:53,731 --> 00:08:56,073
and a monthly maintenance fee appears,

274
00:08:56,073 --> 00:08:58,969
often somewhere around five to fifteen dollars.

275
00:08:59,149 --> 00:09:01,233
Twelve dollars a month is one hundred

276
00:09:01,233 --> 00:09:02,697
forty four dollars a year,

277
00:09:02,697 --> 00:09:04,443
for an account that may pay you

278
00:09:04,443 --> 00:09:05,964
almost nothing in interest.

279
00:09:06,314 --> 00:09:07,834
Then there are service fees.

280
00:09:08,014 --> 00:09:10,730
Wire transfers, which can cost twenty five

281
00:09:10,730 --> 00:09:11,764
dollars or more.

282
00:09:11,944 --> 00:09:13,696
Out of network ATM fees,

283
00:09:13,696 --> 00:09:15,447
sometimes charged twice,

284
00:09:15,447 --> 00:09:17,564
once by your bank and once by

285
00:09:17,564 --> 00:09:18,585
the ATM owner.

286
00:09:18,765 --> 00:09:20,354
Paper statement fees,

287
00:09:20,354 --> 00:09:23,379
foreign transaction fees on debit cards,

288
00:09:23,379 --> 00:09:26,177
and fees for cashier's checks or stop

289
00:09:26,177 --> 00:09:26,858
payments.

290
00:09:27,208 --> 00:09:28,833
Each fee is small.

291
00:09:29,013 --> 00:09:31,216
But fees are almost pure profit for

292
00:09:31,216 --> 00:09:33,672
a bank, because the service often costs

293
00:09:33,672 --> 00:09:35,120
very little to provide.

294
00:09:35,300 --> 00:09:38,163
Try this: download your last three statements

295
00:09:38,163 --> 00:09:39,944
and search for the word fee.

296
00:09:40,124 --> 00:09:41,726
Add up everything you find.

297
00:09:41,906 --> 00:09:43,871
Then ask your bank to waive them,

298
00:09:43,871 --> 00:09:45,777
or switch to a bank that doesn't

299
00:09:45,777 --> 00:09:46,492
charge them.

300
00:09:46,842 --> 00:09:48,014
Way number six.

301
00:09:48,194 --> 00:09:49,924
Your debit card swipes.

302
00:09:50,274 --> 00:09:52,412
Every time you use your debit card,

303
00:09:52,412 --> 00:09:53,879
the merchant pays a fee,

304
00:09:53,879 --> 00:09:55,529
and part of it goes to your

305
00:09:55,529 --> 00:09:55,835
bank.

306
00:09:56,015 --> 00:09:57,781
Since the Durbin amendment,

307
00:09:57,781 --> 00:09:59,678
part of the Dodd Frank law in

308
00:09:59,678 --> 00:10:02,426
twenty ten, the Federal Reserve has capped

309
00:10:02,426 --> 00:10:04,716
these fees for large banks at about

310
00:10:04,716 --> 00:10:07,333
twenty one cents plus a small percentage

311
00:10:07,333 --> 00:10:08,379
per transaction.

312
00:10:08,559 --> 00:10:11,104
Smaller banks and credit unions are exempt

313
00:10:11,104 --> 00:10:11,891
from the cap.

314
00:10:12,242 --> 00:10:14,707
A few cents per swipe doesn't sound

315
00:10:14,707 --> 00:10:15,411
like much.

316
00:10:15,591 --> 00:10:18,040
But Americans make tens of billions of

317
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:20,038
debit card payments every year.

318
00:10:20,218 --> 00:10:22,196
That adds up to billions of dollars

319
00:10:22,196 --> 00:10:22,818
in revenue.

320
00:10:22,998 --> 00:10:25,522
This is one reason banks encourage you

321
00:10:25,522 --> 00:10:27,714
to use your debit card instead of

322
00:10:27,714 --> 00:10:30,171
cash, and why some accounts require a

323
00:10:30,171 --> 00:10:32,895
certain number of card purchases to avoid

324
00:10:32,895 --> 00:10:33,227
fees.

325
00:10:33,577 --> 00:10:35,666
Should you avoid your debit card because

326
00:10:35,666 --> 00:10:36,084
of this?

327
00:10:36,264 --> 00:10:37,402
Not necessarily.

328
00:10:37,582 --> 00:10:39,384
The fee is paid by the merchant,

329
00:10:39,384 --> 00:10:39,834
not you.

330
00:10:40,014 --> 00:10:42,552
But it's worth knowing that for everyday

331
00:10:42,552 --> 00:10:44,898
purchases, a credit card paid in full

332
00:10:44,898 --> 00:10:47,562
each month often gives you more protection

333
00:10:47,562 --> 00:10:50,100
and rewards, while the debit card mainly

334
00:10:50,100 --> 00:10:51,241
benefits the bank.

335
00:10:51,421 --> 00:10:53,645
Use whichever keeps you in control of

336
00:10:53,645 --> 00:10:54,486
your spending.

337
00:10:54,837 --> 00:10:55,881
Way number seven.

338
00:10:56,061 --> 00:10:57,571
Selling you more products.

339
00:10:57,921 --> 00:10:59,371
Once you're a customer,

340
00:10:59,371 --> 00:11:01,137
banks want to sell you more.

341
00:11:01,317 --> 00:11:02,368
A credit card,

342
00:11:02,368 --> 00:11:04,920
a mortgage, an investment account,

343
00:11:04,920 --> 00:11:06,947
insurance, a personal loan.

344
00:11:07,127 --> 00:11:08,397
Inside the industry,

345
00:11:08,397 --> 00:11:10,238
this is called cross selling,

346
00:11:10,238 --> 00:11:11,572
and it's a big focus,

347
00:11:11,572 --> 00:11:14,111
because a customer with five products is

348
00:11:14,111 --> 00:11:16,651
more profitable and less likely to leave

349
00:11:16,651 --> 00:11:18,238
than a customer with one.

350
00:11:18,588 --> 00:11:20,724
Cross selling isn't bad on its own.

351
00:11:20,904 --> 00:11:22,262
But it can go very wrong.

352
00:11:22,442 --> 00:11:23,615
In twenty sixteen,

353
00:11:23,615 --> 00:11:26,288
regulators fined Wells Fargo after it was

354
00:11:26,288 --> 00:11:27,852
revealed that employees,

355
00:11:27,852 --> 00:11:29,612
under heavy sales pressure,

356
00:11:29,612 --> 00:11:32,871
had opened millions of accounts without customers'

357
00:11:32,871 --> 00:11:33,588
permission.

358
00:11:33,768 --> 00:11:36,740
The bank paid large penalties and settlements.

359
00:11:36,920 --> 00:11:38,884
It became one of the most famous

360
00:11:38,884 --> 00:11:40,786
banking scandals of the decade.

361
00:11:41,136 --> 00:11:44,131
The investment and advice side can also

362
00:11:44,131 --> 00:11:45,129
be expensive.

363
00:11:45,309 --> 00:11:48,338
Some bank investment products come with higher

364
00:11:48,338 --> 00:11:51,300
fees than similar low cost options elsewhere.

365
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:53,727
Try this: when your bank offers a

366
00:11:53,727 --> 00:11:55,973
new product, ask three questions.

367
00:11:56,153 --> 00:11:57,746
What does it cost per year in

368
00:11:57,746 --> 00:11:58,185
dollars?

369
00:11:58,365 --> 00:12:00,513
How is the person recommending it paid?

370
00:12:00,693 --> 00:12:02,038
And can I get the same thing

371
00:12:02,038 --> 00:12:03,142
cheaper somewhere else?

372
00:12:03,493 --> 00:12:04,433
Way number eight.

373
00:12:04,613 --> 00:12:05,089
Float.

374
00:12:05,439 --> 00:12:08,208
When money moves between accounts or banks,

375
00:12:08,208 --> 00:12:09,560
there can be a delay.

376
00:12:09,740 --> 00:12:12,292
Deposits sometimes take a day or two

377
00:12:12,292 --> 00:12:13,710
to become available,

378
00:12:13,710 --> 00:12:15,766
and some transfers take days.

379
00:12:15,946 --> 00:12:16,907
During that time,

380
00:12:16,907 --> 00:12:18,661
the money is sitting somewhere,

381
00:12:18,661 --> 00:12:20,810
and banks can benefit from holding it.

382
00:12:20,990 --> 00:12:22,976
This is sometimes called float.

383
00:12:23,325 --> 00:12:25,997
Faster payment systems are shrinking float,

384
00:12:25,997 --> 00:12:27,737
which is good for customers.

385
00:12:27,917 --> 00:12:30,794
The Federal Reserve launched an instant payment

386
00:12:30,794 --> 00:12:33,548
service called FedNow in twenty twenty three,

387
00:12:33,548 --> 00:12:35,630
and many banks now offer real time

388
00:12:35,630 --> 00:12:36,242
transfers.

389
00:12:36,422 --> 00:12:38,640
But holds on deposits still exist,

390
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:41,576
often for good reasons like fraud prevention.

391
00:12:41,756 --> 00:12:43,924
Knowing how long your bank holds deposits

392
00:12:43,924 --> 00:12:45,669
helps you avoid bounced payments.

393
00:12:46,019 --> 00:12:49,092
Try this: check your bank's funds availability

394
00:12:49,092 --> 00:12:49,560
policy.

395
00:12:49,740 --> 00:12:51,504
It's usually on their website.

396
00:12:51,684 --> 00:12:54,267
If your paycheck lands on Friday but

397
00:12:54,267 --> 00:12:56,348
isn't available until Monday,

398
00:12:56,348 --> 00:12:58,644
schedule your bills accordingly,

399
00:12:58,644 --> 00:13:01,084
or ask about early direct deposit,

400
00:13:01,084 --> 00:13:04,026
which some banks and credit unions offer.

401
00:13:04,376 --> 00:13:06,283
So that's how your dollar earns money

402
00:13:06,283 --> 00:13:06,953
for the bank.

403
00:13:07,133 --> 00:13:08,656
The spread on loans,

404
00:13:08,656 --> 00:13:10,560
the low rate on deposits,

405
00:13:10,560 --> 00:13:12,160
the loans themselves,

406
00:13:12,160 --> 00:13:15,282
overdraft fees, account and service fees,

407
00:13:15,282 --> 00:13:16,653
debit card swipes,

408
00:13:16,653 --> 00:13:18,557
cross selling, and float.

409
00:13:18,737 --> 00:13:20,363
Most of these are reasonable.

410
00:13:20,543 --> 00:13:21,994
A few are expensive.

411
00:13:22,174 --> 00:13:23,804
And almost all of them can be

412
00:13:23,804 --> 00:13:25,715
reduced if you know where to look.

413
00:13:26,064 --> 00:13:27,996
One more thing worth knowing about the

414
00:13:27,996 --> 00:13:28,352
spread.

415
00:13:28,532 --> 00:13:30,968
When interest rates in the economy rise,

416
00:13:30,968 --> 00:13:33,344
banks often raise the rates they charge

417
00:13:33,344 --> 00:13:34,379
on loans quickly,

418
00:13:34,379 --> 00:13:36,267
but raise the rates they pay on

419
00:13:36,267 --> 00:13:37,181
savings slowly.

420
00:13:37,361 --> 00:13:38,502
When rates fall,

421
00:13:38,502 --> 00:13:40,356
the reverse often happens.

422
00:13:40,536 --> 00:13:42,343
Savings rates drop fast,

423
00:13:42,343 --> 00:13:45,204
and loan rates drift down more slowly.

424
00:13:45,384 --> 00:13:48,309
Economists call this deposit rate stickiness.

425
00:13:48,489 --> 00:13:50,707
For savers, the lesson is simple.

426
00:13:50,887 --> 00:13:52,500
Don't wait for your bank to give

427
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:53,104
you a raise.

428
00:13:53,284 --> 00:13:55,455
Go where the rate already is.

429
00:13:55,805 --> 00:13:57,697
So if the numbers are this clear,

430
00:13:57,697 --> 00:13:59,474
why do so many people stay with

431
00:13:59,474 --> 00:14:01,309
a bank that pays them so little?

432
00:14:01,489 --> 00:14:02,452
That's angle two.

433
00:14:02,802 --> 00:14:03,685
Angle two.

434
00:14:03,865 --> 00:14:05,014
The psychology.

435
00:14:05,194 --> 00:14:07,376
Six reasons we keep our money where

436
00:14:07,376 --> 00:14:08,561
it earns the least.

437
00:14:08,911 --> 00:14:10,223
Reason number nine.

438
00:14:10,403 --> 00:14:11,146
Inertia.

439
00:14:11,496 --> 00:14:13,663
Many people still bank where their parents

440
00:14:13,663 --> 00:14:15,055
opened their first account,

441
00:14:15,055 --> 00:14:16,860
or where they signed up in college.

442
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:19,153
Switching feels like a hassle.

443
00:14:19,333 --> 00:14:21,195
Moving direct deposits,

444
00:14:21,195 --> 00:14:23,461
updating automatic payments,

445
00:14:23,461 --> 00:14:24,999
learning a new app.

446
00:14:25,179 --> 00:14:27,917
Economists call the tendency to stick with

447
00:14:27,917 --> 00:14:29,742
the default status quo bias.

448
00:14:29,922 --> 00:14:31,599
Banks know that once you're in,

449
00:14:31,599 --> 00:14:32,952
you're unlikely to leave.

450
00:14:33,301 --> 00:14:35,182
And inertia is profitable.

451
00:14:35,362 --> 00:14:37,831
It's why banks can pay loyal customers

452
00:14:37,831 --> 00:14:39,066
less than new ones.

453
00:14:39,246 --> 00:14:41,641
Some banks even offer better rates to

454
00:14:41,641 --> 00:14:43,971
new customers than to existing ones.

455
00:14:44,151 --> 00:14:45,468
Loyalty, in banking,

456
00:14:45,468 --> 00:14:47,971
is often rewarded with lower interest.

457
00:14:48,320 --> 00:14:50,102
Try this: you don't have to leave

458
00:14:50,102 --> 00:14:50,642
your bank.

459
00:14:50,822 --> 00:14:53,053
Keep your checking account where it is

460
00:14:53,053 --> 00:14:55,284
for convenience, and open a high yield

461
00:14:55,284 --> 00:14:58,160
savings account elsewhere for your emergency fund

462
00:14:58,160 --> 00:14:59,216
and savings goals.

463
00:14:59,396 --> 00:15:00,186
Link them.

464
00:15:00,366 --> 00:15:02,351
It takes about fifteen minutes and can

465
00:15:02,351 --> 00:15:04,232
earn you hundreds of dollars a year.

466
00:15:04,582 --> 00:15:05,767
Reason number ten.

467
00:15:05,947 --> 00:15:07,421
Free feels good.

468
00:15:07,771 --> 00:15:10,372
Researchers have found that people react to

469
00:15:10,372 --> 00:15:12,609
a price of zero very differently from

470
00:15:12,609 --> 00:15:13,576
any other price.

471
00:15:13,756 --> 00:15:16,239
Free checking, free transfers,

472
00:15:16,239 --> 00:15:17,646
free debit cards.

473
00:15:17,826 --> 00:15:19,416
It feels like a great deal.

474
00:15:19,596 --> 00:15:21,148
And it makes us less likely to

475
00:15:21,148 --> 00:15:22,441
ask what we're giving up.

476
00:15:22,621 --> 00:15:25,257
In banking, free often means paid for

477
00:15:25,257 --> 00:15:26,254
in other ways,

478
00:15:26,254 --> 00:15:28,748
like low deposit rates or fees when

479
00:15:28,748 --> 00:15:29,389
you slip.

480
00:15:29,739 --> 00:15:31,805
Try this: look at your bank as

481
00:15:31,805 --> 00:15:32,700
a total cost.

482
00:15:32,880 --> 00:15:33,905
Add up fees paid,

483
00:15:33,905 --> 00:15:35,713
then subtract interest earned.

484
00:15:35,893 --> 00:15:37,377
If the number is a cost to

485
00:15:37,377 --> 00:15:39,318
you, your free account isn't free.

486
00:15:39,498 --> 00:15:41,373
Compare it with what a high yield

487
00:15:41,373 --> 00:15:43,475
account and a no fee checking account

488
00:15:43,475 --> 00:15:44,327
would give you.

489
00:15:44,677 --> 00:15:46,976
Free also shapes how we judge new

490
00:15:46,976 --> 00:15:47,533
options.

491
00:15:47,713 --> 00:15:49,872
A high yield account that pays four

492
00:15:49,872 --> 00:15:51,783
percent might ask you to open a

493
00:15:51,783 --> 00:15:54,003
new login, wait a day for transfers,

494
00:15:54,003 --> 00:15:55,051
or link accounts.

495
00:15:55,231 --> 00:15:57,524
Those small hassles feel bigger than the

496
00:15:57,524 --> 00:15:59,990
hundreds of dollars of interest you'd earn,

497
00:15:59,990 --> 00:16:02,226
because the hassle is immediate and the

498
00:16:02,226 --> 00:16:03,602
interest arrives slowly.

499
00:16:03,782 --> 00:16:05,186
Recognize that bias,

500
00:16:05,186 --> 00:16:07,644
and do the fifteen minutes of setup

501
00:16:07,644 --> 00:16:08,136
anyway.

502
00:16:08,485 --> 00:16:09,670
Reason number eleven.

503
00:16:09,850 --> 00:16:11,371
Big feels safe.

504
00:16:11,720 --> 00:16:14,051
Many people believe a big bank with

505
00:16:14,051 --> 00:16:15,982
a famous name is safer than a

506
00:16:15,982 --> 00:16:17,514
smaller or online bank.

507
00:16:17,694 --> 00:16:19,116
But for most savers,

508
00:16:19,116 --> 00:16:21,746
the key protection is FDIC insurance.

509
00:16:21,926 --> 00:16:24,669
Deposits at insured banks are protected up

510
00:16:24,669 --> 00:16:27,347
to two hundred fifty thousand dollars per

511
00:16:27,347 --> 00:16:29,176
depositor, per insured bank,

512
00:16:29,176 --> 00:16:31,527
for each account ownership category.

513
00:16:31,707 --> 00:16:34,333
Credit unions have similar protection through the

514
00:16:34,333 --> 00:16:36,316
National Credit Union Administration.

515
00:16:36,666 --> 00:16:38,602
In March twenty twenty three,

516
00:16:38,602 --> 00:16:42,072
Silicon Valley Bank collapsed after customers rushed

517
00:16:42,072 --> 00:16:43,674
to withdraw their money,

518
00:16:43,674 --> 00:16:45,743
in one of the fastest bank runs

519
00:16:45,743 --> 00:16:46,477
in history.

520
00:16:46,657 --> 00:16:49,183
Many of its customers had balances far

521
00:16:49,183 --> 00:16:50,778
above the insured limit.

522
00:16:50,958 --> 00:16:53,642
The lesson for ordinary savers isn't that

523
00:16:53,642 --> 00:16:54,755
banks are unsafe.

524
00:16:54,935 --> 00:16:56,869
It's that the insurance limit matters,

525
00:16:56,869 --> 00:16:58,651
and that you should know which bank

526
00:16:58,651 --> 00:17:00,229
is actually holding your money.

527
00:17:00,579 --> 00:17:03,214
Try this: confirm that any bank you

528
00:17:03,214 --> 00:17:04,720
use is FDIC insured,

529
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:07,430
or any credit union is NCUA insured,

530
00:17:07,430 --> 00:17:10,367
using the official government websites.

531
00:17:10,547 --> 00:17:12,171
And if you ever have more than

532
00:17:12,171 --> 00:17:13,850
the insured limit in one place,

533
00:17:13,850 --> 00:17:16,016
ask about how to structure your accounts

534
00:17:16,016 --> 00:17:16,990
to stay protected.

535
00:17:17,340 --> 00:17:18,583
Reason number twelve.

536
00:17:18,763 --> 00:17:19,761
Convenience.

537
00:17:20,111 --> 00:17:22,735
Having everything in one place feels simple.

538
00:17:22,915 --> 00:17:25,756
Checking, savings, credit card,

539
00:17:25,756 --> 00:17:28,047
mortgage, all in one app.

540
00:17:28,227 --> 00:17:31,534
Banks encourage this with relationship discounts and

541
00:17:31,534 --> 00:17:32,615
bundled benefits.

542
00:17:32,795 --> 00:17:35,013
Sometimes the bundle is a good deal.

543
00:17:35,193 --> 00:17:37,403
But often, convenience is the price you

544
00:17:37,403 --> 00:17:38,989
pay for not shopping around.

545
00:17:39,339 --> 00:17:41,330
The best rate on a savings account

546
00:17:41,330 --> 00:17:43,086
is rarely at the same place as

547
00:17:43,086 --> 00:17:44,432
the best mortgage rate,

548
00:17:44,432 --> 00:17:46,305
or the best credit card for you.

549
00:17:46,485 --> 00:17:49,154
Splitting your banking across two or three

550
00:17:49,154 --> 00:17:51,696
institutions takes a little more effort,

551
00:17:51,696 --> 00:17:53,666
but it can mean better rates on

552
00:17:53,666 --> 00:17:54,810
almost everything.

553
00:17:54,990 --> 00:17:57,277
Modern apps make linking accounts easy.

554
00:17:57,626 --> 00:17:59,983
A simple setup works for most people.

555
00:18:00,163 --> 00:18:02,930
One checking account for bills and spending,

556
00:18:02,930 --> 00:18:04,691
wherever is most convenient.

557
00:18:04,871 --> 00:18:07,294
One high yield savings account for the

558
00:18:07,294 --> 00:18:08,888
emergency fund and goals.

559
00:18:09,068 --> 00:18:10,914
And one investment account,

560
00:18:10,914 --> 00:18:12,554
at a low cost brokerage,

561
00:18:12,554 --> 00:18:13,921
for long term money.

562
00:18:14,101 --> 00:18:15,959
Three accounts, three jobs,

563
00:18:15,959 --> 00:18:18,161
each at the place that does that

564
00:18:18,161 --> 00:18:18,780
job best.

565
00:18:19,129 --> 00:18:20,732
Reason number thirteen.

566
00:18:20,912 --> 00:18:21,806
Complexity.

567
00:18:22,155 --> 00:18:24,957
Bank accounts come with long fee schedules,

568
00:18:24,957 --> 00:18:26,326
terms and conditions,

569
00:18:26,326 --> 00:18:27,368
and disclosures.

570
00:18:27,548 --> 00:18:28,895
Few people read them.

571
00:18:29,075 --> 00:18:31,002
That's not because people are lazy.

572
00:18:31,182 --> 00:18:33,197
It's because the documents are long and

573
00:18:33,197 --> 00:18:33,713
confusing.

574
00:18:33,893 --> 00:18:35,770
And when something is confusing,

575
00:18:35,770 --> 00:18:37,353
we stick with what we have.

576
00:18:37,533 --> 00:18:39,762
Complexity protects the status quo.

577
00:18:40,112 --> 00:18:42,201
Try this: you only need to find

578
00:18:42,201 --> 00:18:44,559
five things in any account's terms.

579
00:18:44,739 --> 00:18:45,854
The interest rate.

580
00:18:46,034 --> 00:18:47,987
The monthly fee and how to avoid

581
00:18:47,987 --> 00:18:48,170
it.

582
00:18:48,350 --> 00:18:49,929
The overdraft policy.

583
00:18:50,109 --> 00:18:51,142
The ATM fees.

584
00:18:51,322 --> 00:18:53,365
And the funds availability policy.

585
00:18:53,545 --> 00:18:54,521
Five numbers.

586
00:18:54,701 --> 00:18:55,618
Ten minutes.

587
00:18:55,798 --> 00:18:58,240
That's all it takes to compare almost

588
00:18:58,240 --> 00:18:59,362
any two accounts.

589
00:18:59,712 --> 00:19:02,477
Banks are required to disclose fees and

590
00:19:02,477 --> 00:19:05,738
rates, and many publish simple summary sheets.

591
00:19:05,918 --> 00:19:08,172
Some even offer a one page fee

592
00:19:08,172 --> 00:19:08,623
table.

593
00:19:08,803 --> 00:19:09,595
Ask for it,

594
00:19:09,595 --> 00:19:12,258
or search the bank's site for account

595
00:19:12,258 --> 00:19:13,122
disclosures.

596
00:19:13,302 --> 00:19:14,371
Once you have it,

597
00:19:14,371 --> 00:19:16,195
the comparison takes minutes,

598
00:19:16,195 --> 00:19:18,585
and the complexity barrier disappears.

599
00:19:18,934 --> 00:19:20,641
Reason number fourteen.

600
00:19:20,821 --> 00:19:22,621
You never see what you didn't earn.

601
00:19:22,970 --> 00:19:24,619
Fees show up on your statement.

602
00:19:24,799 --> 00:19:26,695
But the interest you could have earned

603
00:19:26,695 --> 00:19:28,642
somewhere else never shows up anywhere.

604
00:19:28,822 --> 00:19:30,617
There's no line that says you missed

605
00:19:30,617 --> 00:19:32,212
seven hundred dollars this year.

606
00:19:32,392 --> 00:19:34,263
Because the cost is invisible,

607
00:19:34,263 --> 00:19:35,573
it doesn't feel real.

608
00:19:35,753 --> 00:19:38,047
Economists call it opportunity cost,

609
00:19:38,047 --> 00:19:40,150
and it's one of the hardest costs

610
00:19:40,150 --> 00:19:41,488
for humans to notice.

611
00:19:41,838 --> 00:19:43,766
Try this: make it visible.

612
00:19:43,946 --> 00:19:45,978
Write down your savings balance and your

613
00:19:45,978 --> 00:19:46,639
current rate.

614
00:19:46,819 --> 00:19:48,819
Then calculate what you'd earn at a

615
00:19:48,819 --> 00:19:50,418
competitive high yield rate.

616
00:19:50,598 --> 00:19:53,013
The difference is your yearly loyalty cost.

617
00:19:53,193 --> 00:19:55,106
Seeing that number is often enough to

618
00:19:55,106 --> 00:19:55,933
make the switch.

619
00:19:56,283 --> 00:19:57,827
Do the same for loans.

620
00:19:58,007 --> 00:19:59,404
If you have a car loan or

621
00:19:59,404 --> 00:20:01,080
credit card at your main bank,

622
00:20:01,080 --> 00:20:03,315
check what other lenders would offer you

623
00:20:03,315 --> 00:20:03,650
today.

624
00:20:03,830 --> 00:20:05,502
If your credit has improved,

625
00:20:05,502 --> 00:20:07,832
a refinance or a balance transfer might

626
00:20:07,832 --> 00:20:08,787
lower your rate.

627
00:20:08,967 --> 00:20:10,785
Your bank won't call you to suggest

628
00:20:10,785 --> 00:20:10,941
it.

629
00:20:11,121 --> 00:20:13,159
Their best outcome is that you never

630
00:20:13,159 --> 00:20:13,385
ask.

631
00:20:13,735 --> 00:20:15,941
Let's follow one paycheck through the system.

632
00:20:16,121 --> 00:20:19,125
Your employer sends three thousand dollars by

633
00:20:19,125 --> 00:20:20,127
direct deposit.

634
00:20:20,307 --> 00:20:23,014
Within seconds, it's in your checking account,

635
00:20:23,014 --> 00:20:24,544
earning little or nothing.

636
00:20:24,724 --> 00:20:26,903
Your bank now has three thousand more

637
00:20:26,903 --> 00:20:28,846
dollars of deposits it can use to

638
00:20:28,846 --> 00:20:30,436
fund loans and investments.

639
00:20:30,786 --> 00:20:32,630
Over the next two weeks,

640
00:20:32,630 --> 00:20:33,628
you pay rent,

641
00:20:33,628 --> 00:20:36,547
buy groceries with your debit card and

642
00:20:36,547 --> 00:20:37,776
pay a few bills.

643
00:20:37,956 --> 00:20:40,244
Each debit purchase sends a few cents

644
00:20:40,244 --> 00:20:40,986
to the bank.

645
00:20:41,166 --> 00:20:43,134
If your balance dips too low on

646
00:20:43,134 --> 00:20:44,023
the wrong day,

647
00:20:44,023 --> 00:20:45,229
a fee might appear.

648
00:20:45,409 --> 00:20:47,887
Meanwhile, the money you haven't spent yet

649
00:20:47,887 --> 00:20:50,483
is helping the bank earn interest elsewhere.

650
00:20:50,832 --> 00:20:53,010
Now imagine you also carry a credit

651
00:20:53,010 --> 00:20:54,876
card balance at the same bank,

652
00:20:54,876 --> 00:20:56,742
and you have a car loan there.

653
00:20:56,922 --> 00:20:59,117
The bank is earning on your deposits,

654
00:20:59,117 --> 00:21:01,194
on your card balance at over twenty

655
00:21:01,194 --> 00:21:02,737
percent, on your car loan,

656
00:21:02,737 --> 00:21:03,865
and on your swipes.

657
00:21:04,045 --> 00:21:05,474
You might be one of its most

658
00:21:05,474 --> 00:21:08,179
profitable customers, while earning almost nothing on

659
00:21:08,179 --> 00:21:08,944
your own money.

660
00:21:09,294 --> 00:21:10,583
Flip that picture.

661
00:21:10,763 --> 00:21:12,841
Savings in a high yield account,

662
00:21:12,841 --> 00:21:14,594
earning a competitive rate.

663
00:21:14,774 --> 00:21:17,038
Credit card paid in full every month.

664
00:21:17,218 --> 00:21:19,151
Car loan from the cheapest lender you

665
00:21:19,151 --> 00:21:19,726
could find.

666
00:21:19,906 --> 00:21:22,507
Debit or credit card used thoughtfully.

667
00:21:22,687 --> 00:21:24,754
Now the bank still makes some money

668
00:21:24,754 --> 00:21:26,172
from you, which is fair,

669
00:21:26,172 --> 00:21:28,062
but much more of the value flows

670
00:21:28,062 --> 00:21:28,770
back to you.

671
00:21:28,950 --> 00:21:29,705
That's the goal.

672
00:21:30,055 --> 00:21:31,147
Quick pause.

673
00:21:31,327 --> 00:21:33,192
If you're about to go check your

674
00:21:33,192 --> 00:21:34,299
savings rate, good.

675
00:21:34,479 --> 00:21:34,781
Do it.

